#### D.3 Key Terms

**Anticipation Paradox.** The impossibility of proving safety against failure modes not anticipated during design. Formalised in Definition 1.1 as the undecidability of complete safety specification for continuous state spaces.

**Calibrated Novelty ($\tilde{\nu}$).** The product of fused novelty score and detector confidence, ensuring that high disagreement among detectors reduces the effective novelty (conservative behaviour).

**Conservatism Multiplier ($\kappa$).** The exponential scaling factor $10^{\tilde{\nu}}$ that tightens safety margins proportionally to perceived environmental novelty.

**Delta-LTL.** The incremental verification calculus that re-checks only constraints affected by a state delta, achieving polynomial-time complexity.

**Meta-Safety Layer.** The self-referential fourth architectural tier that monitors, verifies, and certifies the safety system itself.

**Proof-Carrying Adaptation.** The protocol requiring every behavioural change to be accompanied by a machine-checkable formal proof, chained into an immutable certificate ledger.

**Safety Context.** The complete tuple $(\pi, \mathcal{M}, \Phi, \Theta)$ capturing the current policy, world model, constraints, and progress measures.

**Shadow Physics.** The lightweight dynamics model used by the ARL to predict next states and detect anomalies via prediction residuals.

**Symmetry Group.** The group $G$ of transformations (translation, rotation, reflection, scale) under which safety constraints are invariant.

